[cerebras-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/ski:
Thinking: We need to answer using the maplibre-cartography skill. Provide code example for smooth flyTo, maybe with easing, options, handling button click, ensuring proper zoom, bearing, pitch, and maybe using flyTo vs easeTo. Also mention accessibility, layer ordering not needed. Provide explanation. Use MapLibre GL JS API. Provide example HTML/JS. Also mention using requestAnimationFrame? Use map.flyTo with options: center, zoom, bearing, pitch, speed, curve, essential. Also mention using map.easeTo for less dramatic. Provide notes about handling button disabled while flying, and about resetting after. Also mention using map.once('moveend') to trigger something. Provide final answer.

Below is a **minimal, production‑ready** pattern that:

1. **Creates a button** (or any clickable element) in the page.  
2. **Calls `map.flyTo()`** when the button is pressed, giving you a smooth, animated transition to a new centre, zoom, bearing and pitch.  
3. **Keeps the UI responsive** – the button is disabled while the animation is running and re‑enabled when the camera settles.  
4. **Works on every MapLibre GL JS version** (≥ 1.13) and respects the map’s `maxZoom` / `minZoom` limits automatically.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – smooth flyTo on button click</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
    .control {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px;
      left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      border-radius: 4px;
      padding: 6px 10px;
      font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
      cursor: pointer;
      user-select: none;
    }
    .control:disabled { opacity:0.5; cursor: not-allowed; }
  </style>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>
<button id="flyBtn" class="control">Fly to Paris</button>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (replace the style URL with yours)
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // or your own style URL
    center: [-74.006, 40.7128], // New York (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0,
    // optional: improve label legibility on imagery (if you ever switch basemap)
    // text-color: "#fff", text-halo-color: "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)", text-halo-width: 1.2
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Define the target camera parameters
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const target = {
    // Paris – note that MapLibre expects [lon, lat] order
    center: [2.3522, 48.8566],
    zoom: 14,          // zoom level you want after the fly
    bearing: 30,       // rotate the map a little (optional)
    pitch: 45,         // tilt the view (optional)
    // The following three options control the feel of the animation:
    speed: 1.2,        // default 1.2 (higher = faster)
    curve: 1.42,       // default 1.42 (higher = more “curvy”)
    essential: true   // true → respects prefers‑reduced‑motion
  };

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Wire the button → flyTo()
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');

  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    // Disable the button while the animation runs
    btn.disabled = true;

    // Start the smooth flight
    map.flyTo(target);
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Re‑enable the button when the camera stops moving
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // `moveend` fires after any camera animation (flyTo, easeTo, jumpTo…)
  map.once('moveend', () => {
    btn.disabled = false;
  });

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  (Optional) – keep the button disabled if the user prefers
  //     reduced motion.  This respects the WCAG “essential” flag above.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
    btn.disabled = true;
    btn.title = 'Animation disabled – enable “reduce motion” to see it';
  }
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Why `flyTo`?

| Feature | `flyTo` | `easeTo` |
|---------|---------|----------|
| **Smooth “flight” curve** (accelerates, then decelerates) | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Automatic pitch & bearing interpolation** | ✅ | ✅ |
| **Custom speed / curve** | ✅ (speed, curve) | ✅ (duration) |
| **Respects `prefers-reduced-motion`** | ✅ (`essential:true`) | ✅ (same flag) |
| **Good for long‑distance jumps** (e.g., continent‑to‑continent) | ✅ | ✅ (but looks more linear) |

If you only need a short, linear pan/zoom, replace `map.flyTo(target)` with `map.easeTo(target)` and drop `speed`/`curve` (use `duration` instead).

### Tweaking the animation feel

* **`speed`** – multiplier of the default speed (1.2). Larger = faster.  
* **`curve`** – how “curvy” the flight path is. 1.0 is a straight line; 2.0 is a very high arc.  
* **`essential`** – when `true` the animation is suppressed for users who have set *Reduce Motion* in their OS. This is the WCAG‑compliant way to honor accessibility preferences.

```js
map.flyTo({
  center: [lon, lat],
  zoom: 15,
  bearing: 0,
  pitch: 60,
  speed: 0.8,   // slower, more cinematic
  curve: 1.8,   // higher arc
  essential: true
});
```

### Common pitfalls & how the skill helps you avoid them

| Pitfall | How the pattern prevents it |
|---------|-----------------------------|
| **Labels become unreadable on satellite imagery** | The skill’s “Basemap Type Determines Label Colors” table tells you to use a dark halo (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) and white text when you later switch the style to an imagery basemap. |
| **Custom icons disappear after a fly** | If you later add point symbols, remember to load them **before** the first symbol layer (see “Layer Ordering”). The example above only uses the default style, but the same rule applies when you add your own `addImage` icons. |
| **Button spams the animation** | The button is disabled while the camera is moving (`moveend` re‑enables it). |
| **Animation runs for users who prefer reduced motion** | `essential: true` automatically skips the animation for those users. |
| **Missing glyphs for non‑Latin scripts** | If you ever add labels in Arabic, Hebrew, or CJK, follow the “Typography: Glyphs and Font Stacks” section to serve Noto glyphs or enable the local‑ideograph fallback. |

---

#### TL;DR code snippet

```js
// Assuming `map` is already created
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  const btn = event.currentTarget;
  btn.disabled = true;               // prevent double‑clicks
  map.flyTo({
    center: [2.3522, 48.8566],       // lon, lat
    zoom: 14,
    bearing: 30,
    pitch: 45,
    speed: 1.2,
    curve: 1.42,
    essential: true                 // respect prefers‑reduced‑motion
  });
  map.once('moveend', () => btn.disabled = false);
});
```

That’s all you need to give users a buttery‑smooth camera transition when they click a button. Happy mapping!
